at look you're not just you know  you're not just playing around with them  that you're serious about that this is  offer is made in earnest and so you will  offer you will give a deposit when you  make this offer cottage or it's typical  to give a deposit so your deposit and it  will vary the higher the deposit the  better because it's it shows the more  earnest you are the more serious that  you are about actually going through  with this offer contract it's typical  for these deposits to be anywhere  between I've seen you know 1% 2% 3% 4%  5% of the actual home price 3% is what  I'm most familiar with but it doesn't  have to be 3% it's really this is just  an indicator to the seller that you're  serious about this so you're going to  write a check right now along with the  offer contract for let's say say 3% of  the offer price so 3% of $300,000 is  nine $9,000  that's a $9,000 check some places it's  not a percentage some people say okay  you just give $1,000 and give $2,000  whatever it is but this money once again  this is a check that you're writing with  the offer contract this shows that you  are serious about this that if for  whatever reason you don't meet your end  of the contract then the seller might be  able to just keep this money so this  shows so this is this shows that you are  serious this shows that you are serious  right here that they should take you  seriously and that they should go  through the process of trying to I guess  close on this property and we'll talk in  future videos more about what closing is  now you're probably think a look I'm  just not going to buy this outright I've  toured the home I like it I like the  neighborhood it seems to be in good  condition  but I don't know for a fact that it's in  good condition unless I have a chance to  inspect it unless I have a chance to get  some experts in there and make sure that  the house is in good condition and to  make sure of that it's typical to throw  some contingencies in your offer  contract contingencies contingencies now  the most typical contingencies are  things like inspections so you might  want to inspect for termites you want to  inspect the foundation you want to  inspect the plumbing the electrical and  make sure that it's all the up-and-up if  some if something comes up in the  inspect  that's you know makes this house not  what you thought it was it allows you to  get out of this contract and hopefully  get your deposit back now other things  that you might want to put a contingency  on well you think that you're going to  be able to get $300,000 maybe you have  saved enough for a deposit you save  let's say 20% of this so you have saved  say $60,000 or maybe you save 60 maybe  save 70 or 80 thousand dollars because  you also need some extra money for  closing costs and whatever else and  we'll talk about that in future videos  and maybe to buy furniture also you save  sick you save 70 or 80 thousand dollars  but the remainder of it you're going to  need to borrow and you're going to  borrow it from a bank the bank has  written a letter to you saying hey you  know based on your income and your  credit score we we feel we've we've  pre-approved your loan we think you're  going to get the loan but that's not the  same thing as actually getting the loan  something might happen the bank might  realize that they didn't notice  something about you or the finance the  financing conditions might change in  between the time that you got your  pre-approval and the time that you  actually need your loan so another  typical contingency that you might have  is a financing contingency saying look I  really do want to buy this house but I'm  clearly not going to be able to buy it  unless I get some financing and then  other things that might be there are  things like insurance why would you need  a contingency on insurance and this one  is this one's less typical so I'll put a  little asterisks there well there might  be it might be a place 